      "text": "On the LHS, since $n$ can be either positive or negative, we have two cases and two equations.\nIf $n \\geqslant 0$, then $n + 6 = 2 - n$ which implies $2n = -4 \\Rightarrow n = -2$.\nHowever, $n$ cannot be negative, so $n = -2$ is not a solution to this case. Note that $n$ cannot be zero either, so we still cancel $n$ in $n + 6 = 2 - n$.\nIf $n < 0$, then $n + 6 = -(2 - n) = -2 + n$ which implies $2n = -8 \\Rightarrow n = -4$.\nNow we have a solution that is valid. Now substitute $-2$ and $-4$ into the LHS, come to realise that they don’t actually match.\nSince neither solutions satisfy the original equation, there are no solutions for this problem. $\\blacksquare$\n\\boxed{None}",
