      "text": "Starting with $|n+6| = 2-n$, we recognize that $|n+6|$ can be defined by the equations $\\begin{cases} n+6 = 2-n & (1) \\\\ n + 6 = - (2 - n) & (2) \\\\ \\end{cases}$. \n\nBy equation (1), we substitute values into $n$ to get $n+6 = 2-n$ which simplifies to $n+6-2 = n-2$ or $n+4= n - 2$. Solving for $n$, we find $4= -2$ and $n = - 2$. However, substituting back, $-1 + \\ (6) = 2 - (-2)$ totals $5 = 5$, which is true - $n=-2$ works for (1) and (2). Thus, $n=-2$ is a solution, and to be sure, we check those options.\n\nSimilarly, for (2), using $n + 6 = - (2-n)$ we get $= n+\\ (6)$ to mean $2 - n$. Subtracting $2$ from each side of the equation, we get $n = 2$ as a solution, because $n + 6 = 6+2$, and $n+6 = 2 - (2)$. Again, when $n = 2$ we get $4 = 4$, which is true. It's possible then that you could choose $n = 2$ as a solution in this problem.\nTherefore, the solutions are [color=#f00]none[/color] of A. -6 B. -2 C. 6 D. 2 E. not enough information The second answer says $n = 2$ is a solution, and that's what we found. However, checking by the subtraction, we have that we get $2 + 6 =$ $2 - (2) + 6$. However, correcting this, because substituting -2 gives us the value $4-6$ which isn't equal to $2-(-2)$. This implies that our original assumption was wrong, and hence, our conclusion that this is a solution is too. Please let me know if this answer is correct.\n\nI haven't seen a prek question in a year :/\n\nWell, I thought $n=-2$ made sense (assuming it was possible to find a number that could satisfy the equation) but then a friend told me there was another solution to the equation which wasn't listed, but what it is don't know right now\n\nthis answer :)\n\npip tub but the guy in redleo just pee in himself\n\nSorry MysticNumber311, but you needed to post rather than just spam! lol\n\nHe worked it through to make sure it was solved he just removed it without post credit goof",
