      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\(|n + 6| = 2 - n\\), we need to consider the definition of absolute value. The absolute value equation \\(|n + 6| = 2 - n\\) can be split into two cases:\n\n1. \\(n + 6 = 2 - n\\)\n2. \\(n + 6 = -(2 - n)\\)\n\nLet's solve each case separately.\n\n**Case 1: \\(n + 6 = 2 - n\\)**\n\nAdd \\(n\\) to both sides:\n\\[n + n + 6 = 2\\]\n\\[2n + 6 = 2\\]\n\nSubtract 6 from both sides:\n\\[2n = 2 - 6\\]\n\\[2n = -4\\]\n\nDivide both sides by 2:\n\\[n = -2\\]\n\n**Case 2: \\(n + 6 = -(2 - n)\\)**\n\nDistribute the negative sign on the right-hand side:\n\\[n + 6 = -2 + n\\]\n\nSubtract \\(n\\) from both sides:\n\\[6 = -2\\]\n\nThis is a contradiction, so there are no solutions from this case.\n\nTherefore, the only solution is from Case 1, which is \\(n = -2\\).\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{-2}\\).",
